      ",description\": \"Explains how all pieces fit together in a practical training loop—data types used for storage vs computation, dequantisation flow, optimizer configuration, and software engineering aspects such as custom CUDA kernels.\",\n\"subtopics\": [\n\"Storage datatype (NF4) versus compute datatype (BF16/BFloat16)\",\n\"Forward pass: dequantize → matrix multiply → add LoRA contribution\",\n\"Backward pass: gradients flow through dequantized weights but are only accumulated on LoRA matrices\",\n\"Blocksize choices for weights (64) and scales (256)\",\n\"Integrating QLoRA into Hugging Face Transformers\",\\\n\"CUDA kernel design for fast 4‑bit dequantisation\",\\\n\"Memory profiling: where the ≈48 GB budget goes\"\n]\n},\n{\n\"chapter_title\": \"Comparative Experiments: QLoRA vs. Full‑Precision Finetuning\",\n\"description\": \"Details experimental protocols used to verify that QLoRA recovers full 16‑bit performance across model families and tasks.\",\n\"subtopics\": [\n\"Benchmark suite (GLUE/ROBERta‑large, SuperNatInst/T5, MMLU)\",\n\"Model scales evaluated (125 M – 3 B)\",\n\"Baseline configurations: full model finetune vs. 16‑bit LoRA\",\n\"Effect of adapter depth—why placing LoRA in every linear layer matters\",\n\"Hyperparameter search findings for learning rate & batch size\",\n\"Results: accuracy / perplexity parity between NF4 QLoRA and baselines\",\n\"Ablation study comparing NF4 ↔ FP4 ↔ Int4\"\n]\n},\n{\n\"chapter_title\": \"Scaling QLoRA to Very Large LLMs (33 B & 65 B)\",\n\"description\": \"Shows how the same method extends to models that are otherwise impossible to finetune on a single GPU, emphasizing memory accounting and performance recovery.\",\n\"subtopics\": [\n\"Memory budget breakdown before/after QLoRA for 33 B & 65 B\",\n\"Role of Double Quantization in reducing constant storage overhead\",\n\"Paged Optimizer behavior on long sequences\",\n\"Instruction tuning experiments on LLaMA series using Alpaca & FLAN v2\",\n\"Zero‑shot MML